findings.body:
Removes the allowlist and sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the request `Origin` (or `*`) while always sending `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** any malicious site can make credentialed cross-origin requests to this API. The browser will attach the victim's cookies and, because ACAO mirrors the attacker's origin, the attacker can read authenticated responses (session data, account details, tokens).

Native mobile HTTP clients do not rely on CORS; browser/webview callers still need a fixed allowlist. Restore allowlist checks (optionally from config) and only emit credentialed ACAO for known origins. Do not use `*` with credentials — browsers reject that pairing, and reflecting arbitrary origins is the dangerous case here.

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return {};
```
